- **Transit tickets in Apple Wallet or Google Wallet.** Suica and PASMO for Tokyo, OMNY for New York, Oyster for London (in Apple Wallet since 2023), Navigo for Paris on newer iPhones. These work even if your phone is in Airplane Mode — NFC still fires.
- **Airline boarding passes saved to Wallet**, plus a PDF screenshot. Don''t rely on the airline app alone; I''ve watched the United app white-screen in line at SFO.
- **Hotel confirmations, tour vouchers, and rail passes** saved as PDFs in a folder on your phone, and emailed to yourself with a searchable subject line like "TRIP-TOKYO-HOTEL."
- **Google Translate offline language packs.** Japanese is about 120 MB, Spanish similar. Download before you fly.
- **An eSIM, activated before departure.** Airalo, Holafly, and Saily all work; a 5 GB regional Europe plan runs roughly $15-25 for 30 days. Activate on your home Wi-Fi; just don''t switch to it as your data line until you land.

A caveat: offline Google Maps routes transit poorly. It''ll give you driving and walking directions fine, but for subways and buses you need a signal or a dedicated transit app like Citymapper (offline mode is limited) or the local operator app (e.g., Tokyo Metro''s free app, which caches line maps).

## The phone setup that makes unplugging stick

