- One emergency contact back home.
- My travel insurance policy number and the 24-hour assistance line.

Fold it into your passport. If your phone dies, any taxi driver, hotel desk, or cop can read an address. I also keep about $100-200 USD in cash in a separate pocket — enough to get to an airport or a hotel without Apple Pay.

## Navigation without doomscrolling

Here''s the honest tradeoff: the moment you unlock your phone to check a map, you''re two taps from Instagram. The fix is to plan your day in a block, then put the phone away.

My morning routine on the road:

1. Over coffee, open Maps and star the 3-4 places I want to hit.
2. Check the rough transit route for each.
3. Screenshot the route (not just save it — a screenshot survives any app crash).
4. Write the neighborhood names on a receipt or in a small notebook.
5. Put the phone on Do Not Disturb and into my bag.

I''ll pull it out to confirm a turn, then it goes back. On a good day I look at my phone maybe 10 times between 9am and 6pm, and never open a social app. The trip feels twice as long in the best way.

